# Releasing {{ .Container }}

A pushed version tag releases {{ .Container }}: the workflow
`.gitea/workflows/release.yaml` runs `make dist VERSION=<tag>` and attaches
every file directly in `dist/` to the Gitea release of that tag, creating the
release when it is missing: {{ $files }}. Nothing is published anywhere else;
people download the files from the release page.

## Cut a release

1. Make sure main is green and holds what you want to ship.
{{- if $manifest }}
2. Set the version in `{{ $manifest }}` to the one you are about to tag,
   without the `v` (`0.2.0` for `v0.2.0`), and merge that to main: `make dist`
   fails when the tag and `{{ $manifest }}` disagree.
3. Tag main and push the tag:
{{- else }}
2. Tag main and push the tag:
{{- end }}

   ```sh
   git switch main && git pull
   git tag v0.2.0
   git push origin v0.2.0
   ```

   Or create the tag on main in Gitea's web UI. Tags are `vX.Y.Z`; only a
   tag starting with `v` starts the workflow. On a protected repository only
   the operator's Git user may push `v*` tags.
{{- if $manifest }}
4. Watch the run under the repository's **Actions** tab; when it is green,
   the release page lists the files.
{{- else }}
3. Watch the run under the repository's **Actions** tab; when it is green,
   the release page lists the files.
{{- end }}

The workflow runs on a runner labelled `go`, the one the checks use, with the
workflow's own token: no secrets to set.

## Check a release locally

Build the release files the way the workflow does, before you tag:
{{ if $manifest }}
```sh
make dist VERSION=v0.2.0   # the version in {{ $manifest }}, with a v
ls dist/
```

`make dist` checks the version against `{{ $manifest }}`, so a throwaway one
such as `v0.0.0-test` fails there; that failure is the check working.
{{- else }}
```sh
make dist VERSION=v0.0.0-test
ls dist/
```
{{- end }}

Every file directly in `dist/` is what the release would get; folders in
`dist/` are not attached. Remove `dist/` afterwards; it is not committed.

## When a run fails

The run's log says which step failed: `make dist`, or attaching the files.

- **`make dist` failed** (a build error{{ if $manifest }}, or the tag doesn't
  match `{{ $manifest }}`{{ end }}): nothing was attached. Fix it on main, then
  move the tag to the fixed commit and push it again:

  ```sh
  git tag -f v0.2.0 && git push -f origin v0.2.0
  ```

  Or, when the tag was already shared, leave it and tag the fix as the next
  version (`v0.2.1`).
- **Attaching failed** (Gitea was unreachable, or an upload broke halfway):
  re-run the job from the **Actions** tab, or push the tag again. A run
  replaces the files that are already on the release, so running it again
  is safe.
- **A wrong file got attached:** delete the asset on the release page (edit
  the release), fix `make dist`, and run the release again as above. Only
  files that the new run writes are replaced; one it no longer writes stays
  until you delete it.
